Package: mos-board-x64
Version: @VERSION@
Architecture: @ARCH@
Maintainer: mos build <mos@example.invalid>
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Depends: mos-system (= @VERSION@), linux-image-amd64, initramfs-tools, cryptsetup-bin, ${shlibs:Depends}
Description: mos board support -- x64 (QEMU/UEFI, A/B layout)
 Everything the image carries because it is the x64 board: /etc/fstab and the
 ESP mount unit rendered from boards/x64/board.env, the RAUC system
 configuration for its grub backend, the dm-verity initramfs hook and local-top
 script, and /usr/bin/grub-editenv. The ESP's own grub.cfg is not in here: the
 image assembler renders it from the repository, so a device copy would be an
 unrendered template nothing reads.
 .
 There is no vendor BSP behind this board. A UEFI machine's firmware provides
 the boot chain and the kernel is Debian's own, so linux-image-amd64 is a
 dependency rather than a payload -- and the initramfs is built by that
 package's own trigger, from the two initramfs-tools files this package ships.
 .
 initramfs-tools and cryptsetup-bin are named because ELF metadata cannot show
 them: the hook is read by update-initramfs and copies veritysetup into the
 initrd by name, and neither relationship is visible to dpkg-shlibdeps.
 grub-editenv's libdevmapper is not named here: it is an ELF dependency, so
 ${shlibs:Depends} derives it, and with the version dpkg-shlibdeps reads.
